ally aerosolized in the form of  droplets  that are small enough to float in air  so that turned out not to be an issue so  we examined  more closely using mathematica the  direct  uh deposition of the stream  onto the epithelial surface and  diffusion  along the nasal cavity surface  potentially to  the olfactory epithelium now zinc  toxicity  is defined in terms of toxic load which  is the product  of a power of concentration times time  and it was  using available experimental data  able to characterize  the toxic loads uh toxic load magnitudes  that would be likely to kill  olfactory human  neurons and so using that information  we could then look at these two issues  of direct deposition  and diffusion to uh  examine the the likelihood of  olfactory neuron damage  um and the anosmia that that would  cause which is the  loss of the sense of smell  to define the potential for that outcome  quantitatively and so here what we did  to do this was  use a very detailed model published by a  group in canada  in 2009 uh involving uh  a half a million vertex  model a three-dimensional model at a  half a millimeter  resolution uh based on mris taking of 30  uh adults male and female  and the vertices then could be used to  examine  the issue of direct deposition  um here you're seeing the nasal model  uh here you see half of the nasal cavity  which is divided into by a septal wall  uh the middle of the nasal cavity so  here you see the septal wall and then  rotating  we see the lateral wall here  a very complex geometry to the human  nasal cavity  um making this a challenging exercise  but  facilitated by mathematica's 3d  imaging capabilities  and we can see that injecting a  stream of nasal gel  into the nasal cavity in a way that  would  deposit onto the olfactory epithelium  is uh not really possible  because there's a bow shaped curvature  to the septal wall that prevents a  stream of gel from shooting up that far  so we are  then left with the  analysis of zinc concentration  by diffusion over the surface of the  nasal epithelium  as a potential pathway to neuronal  damage  and to do this we take the  five half a million vertices  and slice this model into sections  uh each formed by a smooth  polygon so we have  221 sections  each containing 23 to 277 points  placed at a half millimeter intervals  and together the sections comprising a  a an irregular tubular grid  um  the slices were  were modeled by taking the  mri images uh collapsing the  half millimeter uh set of of points  along each section into a  plane and then modeling the polygon  formed by that set of points this was  done  for all 221 sections uh the sections  become increasingly irregular  toward the middle of the nasal  epithelium as one travels from the front  uh where the uh the  nose hole is uh toward the back uh  where the pharynx is and throat  and using this model then we can uh  define a characteristic placement of  nasal gel  and this is a conservative placement  along  the  wall of the nasal cavity in  two different places but not uh  up to the olfactory epithelium because  we showed that we couldn't directly  inject it  there in a feasible way  but it's conservatively placed insofar  as  the instructions for a nasal gel  generally say to  deposit the material close to the  end of the nose  toward the tip but  then using this placement we can examine  the concentration of zinc that arises by  diffusion  now diffusion occurs  in proportion to the square root of  time and  what we're able to do using uh by  physically modeling the process  diffusion  in a a layer that has the shape of this  uh complicated nasal surface geometry  uh is look at what happens to that  concentration  around the gel as the gel is pushed back  by mucociliary clearance over time  three minutes 10 minutes 20 minutes 60  minutes and here you're seeing the  nasal surface inverted so you're seeing  the apex  at the bottom of this solid  space and our target olfactory  epithelial regions  are shown as blue stripes on the floor  of this  space and the concentration of zinc on a  logarithmic scale  is shown vertically and  what you see is that after the gel is  deposited  the mucociliary clearance generally  avoids nearly all of the olfactory  target areas demonstrating that there is  extremely  low likelihood of significant  olfactory loss due to gel deposition  and and thus low likelihood of aenosmia  as a result  the second area that i have will  illustrate  involves biosurveillance and  [Music]  one of the ways that biosurveillance is  done is by following emergency medical  service events um  that occur in in the united states  and there is a network uh linking  information about these events uh that  are occurring  out of in about half the united states  uh at this point and so the event  specific information is collected on a  near real time basis  and available for analysis  um here you're seeing an ex  as an example a constitutional  syndrome uh uh  occurring in the counties comprising  chicago  uh over 60 to 90 days in the recent past  and uh  what we have here is a summary of how  these kind of  historical patterns of syndromic  occurrence of e m s events  has been characterized for five  different syndromes  and six different locations for urban  areas and two states  um and it's  worth noting that uh syndromes  uh can be apparent evident at the time  of the ems based on the symptoms that  occur  and are observed by the ems personnel  like gi overdose etc  but also syndromes can be  hypothesized based on  symptom symptomatic observations  that are then coded and  posited to be a syndrome such as  the occurrence of covet 19  the source of the the current pandemic  and so here is a  there we go it's a just a summary of the  different kind of historic patterns that  can pertain to these different  locations and syndromes  and these kinds of patterns can be used  to  evaluate whether a recent occurring  event  is anomalous given the history  of similar events in that location  for that syndrome  so there is a  an application of mathematica that's  that's uh  pertinent to biosurveillance and here  uh is a summary of an analysis done to  determine  whether the the hypothesized  um  [Music]  ems events involving the  hypothesized coven 19 syndrome  actually correspond to the cdc reported  incidents of coven 19  in 15 or so different states  [Music]  and all the counties  contained in those states so  we're looking at  weekly occurrences within those counties  and states  cumulative weekly occurrences that are  predicted  using the biosurveillance hypothesized  syndrome of coven 19  versus the cdc reported incidents for  that  county and state and time period  um and  the difference between being that the  ems database allows you near real time  biosurveillance whereas the  cdc incidence is delayed substantially  by the requirement for an actual  diagnosis  after admission to hospital and testing  before the actual incidences  reported out at that level and so you  see  using the hypothesized syndrome you can  get within a factor of five or so  of the actual uh eventually reported  incidents  third is the area of racial disparity  in u.s county level incidents of fatal  police shootings  as summarized by the washington post  police  shooting fatality database covering  2015 through 2020  the mathematica utility here  is that the database uh that's included  uh that that has conv been compiled by  the washington post  includes lat lawn latitude longitude  information for most  events but does not include county  information they have city and state  but not county and to link this  back to census data  a convenient way to do that is to use  u.s census data for  counties during this time period and so  what i did was use mathematica to  interpret the lat lawn data to define  the corresponding counties  for the thousands of rows of data in  these databases  so here you see a summary of incidents  by county per hundred thousand  for unarmed men on on the top figure  here and for  armed men  shot by police on the bottom and so the  both of these patterns  quite clearly show an excess  incidence for black men  compared to white men  this is statistically significant when  analyzed in an odds ratio  manner but here we're looking at  distributions of  incidents and the difference is even  more pronounced  looking at the patterns in this way  particularly for unarmed men where you  see about an eight-fold uh  a difference in in average incidents  and uh then for armed men about a  five-fold  difference uh the odds ratio is about  four  um when analyzed using contingency  tables  using the summary data rather than the  incidents by county  so this is another interesting  application of mathematica  there the mathematica's ability to  interpret lat lawn  positions and and corresponding counties  was a key  utility finally  um mathematica  is something i've used to compare the  plausibility and applicability  of different competing models of cancer  in view of relevant uh experimental  cancer bioassay data  the 75 year old two-stage somatic  mutation  clonal expansion theory of cancer  short or referred to in shorthand as the  somatic mutation  model  views the critical events in cancer to  be  an accumulation of critical  uh mutations of critical oncogenes  so you're damaging dna causing mutations  of critical genes that govern  cell proliferation and  cell suicide are called  apoptosis these are two fundamentally  important processes  that govern cell replication  and oncogenes are known to be associated  with those  two processes  and it's been posited that uh  mutating as few as two critical  oncogenes may be sufficient to generate  a a tumor cell from a normal cell  in  a variety of tissues really  all tissues subject to tumor genesis  and  this model is known more recently as the  mvk model in that  the original model posited shortly after  the structure of dna was  published in 1954 by watson and crick  um um  uh basically laid out this  this uh this interpretation of cancer um  basically used that that information  concerning  dna to infer that as few as  two mutations could lead to  cancer and be consistent with data on  human incidents of cancer  in terms of pattern over time  by allowing for the fact that  pre-cancers or pre-malignant cancers  that have gotten  the first mutation but not the second  could clonally  expand and proliferate before they  acquire the  one of them acquires the second critical  mutation and that generates a pattern  consistent  with most human cancers um  mogadkar uh was the first  and his colleagues uh was the first to  solve the mathematical  version of this model interpreted as a  doubly stochastic  non-homogeneous poisson process  so a process  involving a discrete change and a birth  death  process that allows for  a pre-malignant clonal expansion  followed by a second  critical required change  where the critical mutations are modeled  as poisson events  and you have a birth death process  separating those events  so uh that's not the only theory of  cancer though there have  been a number of theories  that are competed the somatic mutation  is the dominant theory today  it is the behind most of funded cancer  research  but it has been well recognized for many  uh decades that inflammation  plays a critical role in cancer  and an alternative model uh that i have  uh worked on over the last uh  seven ten years or so uh is summarized  here  uh it's something i call the  inflammation somatic mutation  model very similar but here's the  traditional model laid  out vertically you have the two  mutations and a birth  death process here birth and death  uh this this is a subtle but  profound reinterpretation of the model  in that  uh in contrast to the original model  where  any stem cell in any tissue can undergo  these critical changes  and lead to a cancer i am positing that  only a small a normally small subset of  stem cells are relevant  to the process as it typically occurs so  that  cancer is most efficiently formed  only involving a certain subset  of stem cells those that arise  after tissue injury is followed by  inflammation and the inflammatory immune  cells  then work as they are now known to do to  activate local stem cells and actually  orchestrate the process  of tissue repair  which is involves an epigenetic  conversion  not dna damage not mutations but  something that does not involve mutation  per se but affects how genes  are are translated uh  the importance of epigenetics  to biology and cancer was only  recognized  in the 1990s with nobel prizes awarded  in the  mid-2000s  there's a exploding literature showing  how epigenetic dysregulation can  seems to be an important factor in  cancer  this postulates how this could actually  occur  well it turns out  an important rat bioassay study  was published in 2014 by  a group at dartmouth and johns hopkins  studying uh chemo prevention of  aflatoxin-induced liver cancer  so aflatoxin is a  chemical associated with molds of grains  it is a potent carcinogen particularly  to liver  but also other tissues known to be a  potent rat carcinogen and also a highly  potent mutagen  damages dna it binds onto dna  makes it difficult to replicate causes  damage and mutations  so it's always been thought that the  mutations are what cause the  tumors but this group  compared rats exposed to aflatoxin only  when  they are young juveniles for four weeks  um they expose them to enough  uh aflatoxin to give nearly all of them  liver cancer and then they compared  those rats to  a similarly exposed group  exposed to aflatoxin the same way but  also  exposed to a very potent  anti-inflammatory tri-terpenoid agent  and after that they looked at dna damage  and liver tumors  so the amazing thing about this study is  that  compared to all the liver cancer shown  in the aflatoxin exposed group the group  that got the same aflatoxin at the same  time  but also at the early juvenile stage was  also exposed to the anti-inflammatory  agent  got no liver tumors  and the issue is uh well how could that  be unless the  the anti-inflammatory agent prevented  all the mutations uh  how could this be um well in fact they  looked at the  aflatoxin adducts that cause the  mutations and they were not decreased  enough  to explain this by any means  and what i did recently was to  use the the uh doubly stochastic  poisson model of the current  dominant theory of tumor genesis to show  how that model is perfectly consistent  with the  aflatoxin exposed group of rat results  it cannot explain how  the studies observe zero tumors  with the exposure with the co-exposure  to the anti-inflammatory agent  and here you see the background  incidence of  liver tumors in the same kind of rat  studies very rare  you wouldn't see any you wouldn't hope  to see any in about 20 rats  uh you need about 500 rats to see  a liver tumor  and and here what you see  is the binomial upper 95  confidence limit on a proportion of 0  out of twenty  and you see that's the confidence bound  and  the the two stage model the mvk model  assuming it reduced the mutation rates  as much as that which was observed  in the study um was only able to  predict a reduction in liver tumor  incidence  here at the end of life whereas in fact  the rats showed zero liver tumor so  the alternative model that i  proposed predicts instead that by  blocking inflammation  the the anti-inflammatory agent impedes  the activation of tumor relevant stem  cells and that explains the absence of  liver tumors  from this study so four ways of using  mathematica  to investigate uh toxicology and  environmental health  and i'll leave it at that and take  questions  thank you

